{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789053869,"nonce":"NV6I96b8y01UCV+nEIFfnuWh5zg=","message":"Caught it. Run 38 of 60 direct runs on the NUC:\n\n    [ash] uncaught exception: \"Can't call closure whose type is not a function\"\n    [ash]   at utest.Dispatcher.dispatch(utest/Dispatcher.hx:36)\n    [ash]   at utest.ITestHandler.checkTest(utest/ITestHandler.hx:75)\n    [ash]   at utest.Async.done(utest/Async.hx:51)\n    [ash]   at fun$726(utest/Async.hx:97)\n    [ash]   at fun$611(haxe/Timer.hx:149)\n    [ash]   at fun$612(haxe/Timer.hx:81)\n    [ash]   at sys.thread.EventLoop.loop(EventLoop.hx:183)\n    [ash]   at sys.thread._Thread.$Thread_Impl_.processEvents(Thread.hx:83)\n    [ash]   at fun$857(?:1)\n\nThe message is the guard at std/src/fun.rs:665: it reads (*(*cl).t).fun and finds no signature. So a closure's TYPE POINTER is wrong -- the program is not calling a non-function, it is calling something whose type is no longer a function type.  is just the unnamed thread entry, not the culprit.\n\n**Not a GC rooting bug.** ASH_GC_STRESS=1: 0 failures in 60, against 1 in 60 without. Stress does not raise the rate (it perturbs the timing that exposes it, if anything). Do not re-run that experiment.\n\nNote the first attempt at it was worthless: the wasm host forwarded NO environment to the guest, so ASH_GC_STRESS was inert inside the module and five clean runs meant nothing. Fixed separately -- ASH_* now reaches a guest -- and any wasm diagnostic run from before that commit should be discarded.\n\n**Leading hypothesis: the fiber transform's spill/restore, not the GC.** wasm-fiber-transform-state records that the instrumentation is proven INERT (1069/1069 under both arms, no case changing answer) and says in as many words that this \"says nothing about suspending, which needs the runtime half\". The threads suite is the workload that actually suspends, and this failure sits directly on a suspend path: EventLoop.loop runs a timer closure, which suspends and resumes, and a local holding a closure comes back pointing at something whose type is not HFUN. A local of reference type not spilled, or restored from the wrong slot, produces exactly this symptom and nothing else in the system does.\n\nWhere to look: crates/ash_wasm_link/src/fiber.rs and suspend.rs -- the ladder's save sequence, specifically whether every live reference local at a suspend point is in it. The transform gives each function ONE copy of its save sequence branched to from each call site, which is where a missing local would hide.\n\nRate for whoever reproduces: about 1 in 60 direct runs of bin/threads.wasm-fibers.wasm, 1 in 18 through the harness. Native and interp pass; plain wasm cannot be compared because it TIMEOUTs on this suite.","files":null}]}